Presidential elections were held in [[Moldova]] on 16 December 2011. The [[President of Moldova|president]] was elected by the [[Parliament of Moldova|parliament]] in an [[indirect election]], the final such election before the [[Constitutional Court of Moldova|Constitutional Court]] restored the previous system of [[direct election]] by Moldovan citizens. After the election on 16 December failed, a second attempt was made on 15 January 2012.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.actmedia.eu/top+story/the+republic+of+moldova+%3A+marian+lupu,+the+chairman+of+the+parliament+and+the+interim+head+of+the+state,+and+vlad+filat+%96+premier+/31394 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110720153112/http://www.actmedia.eu/top+story/the+republic+of+moldova+:+marian+lupu,+the+chairman+of+the+parliament+and+the+interim+head+of+the+state,+and+vlad+filat+%96+premier+/31394 |url-status=dead |archive-date=20 July 2011 |title=The republic of moldova : marian lupu, the chairman of the parliament and the interim head of the state, and vlad filat – premier |publisher=Actmedia.eu |access-date=16 March 2012 }}</ref><ref name="rferl1">{{cite web|url=https://www.rferl.org/a/moldovan_parties_discuss_presidential_compromise/24438053.html |title=Moldovan Leaders Talk Compromise On President |publisher=Rferl.org |date=30 December 2011 |access-date=16 March 2012}}</ref> However, that vote was annulled as being unconstitutional since it had not been held in a [[secret vote]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.eurasiareview.com/12012012-moldova-presidential-election-postponed/|title=Moldova Presidential Election Postponed|date=12 January 2012}}</ref> On 16 March,<ref name=March>{{cite web|url=https://www.rferl.org/a/moldova_parliament_election/24507831.html |title=Moldovan Parliament Sets Presidential Election For March 16 |publisher=Rferl.org |date=7 March 2012 |access-date=16 March 2012}}</ref> parliament elected [[Nicolae Timofti]] as president by 62 votes out of 101, with the PCRM boycotting the election, putting an end to a political crisis that had lasted since [[April 2009 Moldovan parliamentary election|April 2009]].<ref name="washingtonpost1">{{cite news |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/europe/moldova-elects-pro-european-judge-timofti-as-president-ending-3-years-of-political-deadlock/2012/03/16/gIQAl3s8FS_story.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120317062102/http://www.washingtonpost.com/world/europe/moldova-elects-pro-european-judge-timofti-as-president-ending-3-years-of-political-deadlock/2012/03/16/gIQAl3s8FS_story.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=2012-03-17 |title=Moldova elects pro-European judge Timofti as president, ending 3 years of political deadlock}}</ref>

==Background==
After the [[April 2009 Moldovan parliamentary election|parliamentary election held on 5 April 2009]], the [[Party of Communists of the Republic of Moldova|PCRM]] won 49.48% of the vote and 60 seats, one seat too few to elect a president. [[Vladimir Voronin]], up to then president, was elected [[President of the Moldovan Parliament|Speaker of Parliament]] and also retained the presidency with an [[Acting President of Moldova|interim status]]. Protests and riots by opposition activists amidst [[2009 Moldova civil unrest|civil unrest]] followed in April 2009. PCRM were then unable to secure one additional vote during the [[Moldovan presidential election, May–June 2009|May–June 2009 presidential election]] out of the 41 MPs from the three opposition parties; a snap parliamentary election was necessary. The [[April 2009 Moldovan parliamentary election|parliamentary election of July 2009]] led to the PCRM losing further seats to a [[Alliance for European Integration|coalition of opposition parties]] which gained 53 seats. However, the opposition also failed to obtain enough seats to elect a president, thereby producing more uncertainty. Voronin announced on 2 September that he intended to resign, saying that his position as acting president had become "ambiguous and doubtful."<ref>[https://www.rferl.org/a/Acting_Moldovan_President_Voronin_Reportedly_Resigns/1812942.html "Voronin Resigns As Acting Moldovan President"], [[Radio Free Europe|Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty]], 2 September 2009.</ref> He resigned on 11 September,<ref name="Moldovan acting president resigns">{{cite web|url=http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/2009-09/11/content_12038359.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121102194836/http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/2009-09/11/content_12038359.htm|url-status=dead|archive-date=2 November 2012|title=Moldovan acting president resigns|date=11 September 2009|access-date=13 September 2009|publisher=Xinhua News Agency}}</ref><ref name="Last stop: Moldova's former president resigns">{{cite web|url=http://www.sofiaecho.com/2009/09/11/783284_last-stop-moldovas-former-president-resigns|title=Last stop: Moldova's former president resigns|date=11 September 2009|access-date=13 September 2009|work=[[The Sofia Echo]]}}</ref> sending a letter to the parliamentary secretariat.<ref name="Moldovan president Vladimir Voronin resigns">{{cite web|url=http://thestar.com.my/news/story.asp?file=/2009/9/11/apworld/20090911172506&sec=apworld|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091005091725/http://thestar.com.my/news/story.asp?file=%2F2009%2F9%2F11%2Fapworld%2F20090911172506&sec=apworld|url-status=dead|archive-date=5 October 2009|title=Moldovan president Vladimir Voronin resigns|date=11 September 2009|access-date=13 September 2009|work=[[The Star (Malaysia)|The Star]] (Malaysia)}}</ref>

By a vote of 52 MPs, the post of the President of the Republic of Moldova was declared vacant. Therefore, in accordance with Article 91 of the [[Constitution of Moldova (1994)|constitution]], which provides that "the responsibility of the office shall devolve ad interim to the President of Parliament or the [[Prime Minister of Moldova|Prime Minister]], in that order of priority," [[Mihai Ghimpu]] became the [[interim President of the Republic of Moldova|interim president of the Republic of Moldova]].<ref name="Moldova: Communists to End Rule">{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2009/09/12/world/europe/12briefs-Moldova.html|title=Moldova: Communists to End Rule|date=11 September 2009|access-date=13 September 2009|work=The New York Times}}</ref>

The [[Constitutional Court of Moldova]] confirmed earlier on 17 September, the legitimacy of [[Mihai Ghimpu]]'s position as [[Acting President of Moldova]], which gave him the right to nominate a prime minister. On the same day, Ghimpu signed a decree nominating [[Vlad Filat]] for the office of prime minister.

The [[Commission for constitutional reform in Moldova]] was set up under presidential decree on 1 December in order to resolve the [[constitutional crisis]]. The post of Acting President passed later{{Why?|date=March 2012}} to [[Marian Lupu]], who held it since 30 December 2010.{{Original research inline|date=January 2012}}

==Calendarisation==
Following an interrogation submitted by four deputies of the [[Party of Communists of the Republic of Moldova|PCRM]],<ref>{{cite web |url=http://constcourt.md/RO/05_activitate_jurisdictionala/sesizari/parvenite/2011/3b_24.01.2011.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=2011-02-17 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110722170608/http://constcourt.md/RO/05_activitate_jurisdictionala/sesizari/parvenite/2011/3b_24.01.2011.pdf |archive-date=22 July 2011}}</ref> Moldova's Constitutional Court ruled on 8 February 2011 that the parliament is the only institution which can decide when to hold a new presidential election, since the two-month deadline after the end of the mandate of the previous president, as foreseen by the [[Constitution of Moldova|constitution]] as having expired.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.unimedia.info/?mod=news&id=29687 |title=Curtea Constituțională: Parlamentul va decide când va fi ales șeful statului &#124; UNIMEDIA – portalul de ştiri nr. 1 din Moldova |publisher=Unimedia.info |date=8 February 2011 |access-date=16 March 2012}}</ref>

On 20 October 2011 the Moldovan parliament decided to hold a presidential election on 18 November 2011.<ref>{{cite web|author=Kommentar posten |url=http://derstandard.at/1318726413341/Neuer-Anlauf-fuer-Praesidentenkuer |title=Neuer Anlauf für Präsidentenkür – Moldau – derStandard.at " International |publisher=Derstandard.at |access-date=16 March 2012}}</ref>

==Candidatures==
On 4 November 2011, [[Igor Dodon]] announced that he would quit the Party of Communists along with MPs [[Zinaida Greceanii]] and [[Veronica Abramciuc]]. Dodon said that the three of them would vote for a president who was independent from any party, alliance or other political body.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://emoldova.net/politics/formercommunistMPs.htm |title=Former Communist MPs offer three votes to elect Moldovan president |publisher=Emoldova.net |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120709032132/http://emoldova.net/politics/formercommunistMPs.htm |archive-date=9 July 2012}}</ref> They did so due to the PCRM's to boycott the election, thus making it virtually possible to break the impasse by reaching an agreement with the majority coalition that lacks two votes to pass the election threshold.<ref>{{cite web |agency=Reuters|url=http://www.trust.org/trustlaw/news/moldova-defections-may-break-impasse-on-president |title=Moldova defections may break impasse on president – TrustLaw |publisher=Trust.org |date=4 November 2011 |access-date=16 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120917230821/http://www.trust.org/trustlaw/news/moldova-defections-may-break-impasse-on-president |archive-date=17 September 2012}}</ref>

Nevertheless, on 15 November the deadline for candidates' applications expired with no applications provided.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.rbc.ru/rbcfreenews/20111115035441.shtml |title=Выборы президента Молдавии не состоятся из-за отсутствия кандидатов. – Новости дня – РосБизнесКонсалтинг |language=ru |publisher=Rbc.ru |access-date=16 March 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120403114413/http://www.rbc.ru/rbcfreenews/20111115035441.shtml |archive-date=3 April 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The country's laws do not make provision for a situation where there are no presidential candidates, but the elections are likely to be postponed or cancelled.
Under Moldovan law, the presidential election can be repeated only once. If the second attempt fails, the country must hold early parliamentary elections.<ref>[https://www.rferl.org/a/moldova_presidential_election/24391440.html RFE-RL], 16 November 2011</ref>

Following two failed rounds to elect a president, the AEI had two candidates: [[Veronica Bacalu]], the former deputy governor of the [[National Bank of Moldova]], who was working at the [[World Bank]] and was nominated by the Liberal Democrat Party; and [[Ion Ababii]], the former Minister of Health who was nominated by the Democratic Party.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://politicom.moldova.org/news/two-candidates-for-moldovas-presidential-elections-228644-eng.html |title=Two candidates for Moldova's presidential elections |publisher=Politicom.moldova.org |date=25 February 2012 |access-date=16 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120415071726/http://politicom.moldova.org/news/two-candidates-for-moldovas-presidential-elections-228644-eng.html |archive-date=15 April 2012}}</ref> In the end, the head of the [[Supreme Magistrate Council]] Judge [[Nicolae Timofti]] became the compromise candidate. He said that he would maintain "strategic ties" with the United States, Russia and Germany, as well as worth towards settling the dispute of [[Transnistria]]; and he added that "Moldova must become a bridge between East and West from which it can only win."<ref name="kiev">{{cite web|url=http://www.kyivpost.com/news/world/detail/124382/#ixzz1pH0Lm6Vs |title=Kyiv Post. Independence. Community. Trust – World – Moldova makes new bid to elect president, tension high |date=16 March 2012 |publisher=Kyivpost.com |access-date=16 March 2012}}</ref>

==Election==
Following successive failures to elect a president, the country had an acting president for over 900 days.<ref name=March/> A 60% vote was required to approve a president.

According to Article 80 of the constitution: "The President of Moldova takes office on the oath-taking day and his term has a duration of 4 years."

===First attempt===
[[File:Marian Lupu Senate of Poland.JPG|thumb|150px|[[Marian Lupu]]]]

On 2 December, an attempt was set for 16 December to try to get candidates for the election. At the same time, an electoral law revision was passed that asserted an election will only be held if there are any candidates and only if at least 61 MPs are present at the vote. Should the election again be unsuccessful, an early parliamentary election would have to be held (which would be the third such election after 2009 and 2010).<ref>{{cite web|author=Kommentar posten |url=http://derstandard.at/1322531790658/Dritter-Versuch-zur-Wahl-des-Praesidenten-am-16-Dezember |title=Dritter Versuch zur Wahl des Präsidenten am 16. Dezember – Moldau – derStandard.at " International |publisher=Derstandard.at |access-date=16 March 2012}}</ref>

The three PCRM MPs who defected attended the vote (with the other PCRM MPs boycotting again), but voted against Marian Lupu, who was the only candidate. Another attempt was set for 15 January 2012.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.dw-world.de/dw/article/0,,15606728,00.html |title=Moldova fails to choose a new president &#124; Europe &#124; DW.DE &#124; 16.12.2011 |publisher=Dw-world.de |access-date=16 March 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |author=Associated Press 2011-12-17 12:09&nbsp;am |url=http://www.taiwannews.com.tw/etn/news_content.php?id=1789730 |title=Moldova fails to elect president – Taiwan News Online |publisher=Taiwannews.com.tw |date=17 December 2011 |access-date=16 March 2012 |archive-date=4 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304103250/http://www.taiwannews.com.tw/etn/news_content.php?id=1789730 |url-status=dead }}</ref> However, Lupu announced he would not run in the election, and stated that the governing parties were prepared to nominate a compromise candidate.<ref name="rferl1"/>

===Second attempt===
In December 2011, the Sierra Leone-born [[John Onoje]] signed up to run for president.<ref>[[Amnesty International]], [http://livewire.amnesty.org/2012/02/21/a-racist-moldova/ A racist Moldova?] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131104062938/http://livewire.amnesty.org/2012/02/21/a-racist-moldova/ |date=4 November 2013 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://social.moldova.org/news/moldovan-communist-politician-made-racist-statements-publicly-228547-eng.html |title=Moldovan Communist politician made racist statements publicly |publisher=Social.moldova.org |date=20 February 2012 |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131102080814/http://social.moldova.org/news/moldovan-communist-politician-made-racist-statements-publicly-228547-eng.html |archive-date=2 November 2013}}</ref><ref name="arena1">{{cite web |url=http://arena.md/?go=news&n=9739&t=Dezam%C4%83gire_pentru_John_Onoje___risc%C4%83_s%C4%83_pice_examenul_la_cet%C4%83%C8%9Benie |title=Dezamăgire pentru John Onoje – riscă să pice examenul la cetățenie |publisher=Arena.md |date=14 March 2012 |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120707045823/http://arena.md/?go=news&n=9739&t=Dezam%C4%83gire_pentru_John_Onoje___risc%C4%83_s%C4%83_pice_examenul_la_cet%C4%83%C8%9Benie |archive-date=7 July 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://bucharestherald.com/politics/34-politics/28508-african-man-runs-for-president-of-moldova-wants-unity-with-romania |title=African man runs for president of Moldova, wants unity with Romania |publisher=Bucharestherald.com |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=usurped |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120710181548/http://bucharestherald.com/politics/34-politics/28508-african-man-runs-for-president-of-moldova-wants-unity-with-romania |archive-date=10 July 2012}}</ref> On 9 December, Onoje passed the medical examination in order to run for president.<ref name="arena1"/> Onoje's campaign was so well publicised that the PSRM's [[Victor Șelin]] proposed revoking his Moldovan citizenship on this basis.<ref>{{cite web|author=Un articol de: Gheorghe Budeanu |url=http://www.timpul.md/articol/john-onoje-stiu-ca-rusii-au-ocupat-acest-teritoriu-in-1812-29940.html |title=John Onoje: "Ştiu că ruşii au ocupat acest teritoriu în 1812" |publisher=Timpul.md |date=14 March 2012 |access-date=18 March 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|author=Un articol de: Moni Stănilă Contactează autorul |url=http://www.timpul.md/articol/cum-sa-devii-vizibil-in-rm--29567.html |title=Cum să devii vizibil în RM |publisher=Timpul.md |date=22 February 2011 |access-date=18 March 2012}}</ref>

On 11 January, [[Oazu Nantoi]] told the press that he would run as a candidate and that he had discussed this prospect with Vlad Filat and other leaders of the Alliance for European Integration: "Their reaction was moderately positive. I remain an optimist and I hope that I will secure the parliamentary deputies' support and go ahead with my self-nomination." The same day, the PCRM had proposed the former chairman of the [[National Bank of Moldova]] [[Leonid Tălmaci]] as a compromise candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.kyivpost.com/news/russia/detail/120372/ |title=Moldova remains without presidential candidate so far |date=12 January 2012 |publisher=Kyivpost.com |access-date=18 March 2012}}</ref> The two candidates then registered with the head of the election commission to run for the post of president.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://bsanna-news.ukrinform.ua/newsitem.php?id=18836&lang=en |title=Deadline for submitting registration documents for presidential election expires today |publisher=Bsanna-news.ukrinform.ua |date=12 January 2012 |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120707214707/http://bsanna-news.ukrinform.ua/newsitem.php?id=18836&lang=en |archive-date=7 July 2012}}</ref>

On 12 January, the [[Constitutional Court of Moldova]] annulled the inconclusive December election because some members of parliament violated the law by showing how they marked their ballots on television.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.upi.com/Top_News/World-News/2012/01/12/Court-throws-out-Moldovan-presidental-vote/UPI-40701326396294/?spt=hs&or=tn |title=Court throws out Moldovan presidential vote |publisher=Upi.com |date=12 January 2012 |access-date=18 March 2012}}</ref><ref>[[Radio Free Europe]], [https://www.rferl.org/a/moldova_delays_presidential_vote_in_parliament/24450195.html Moldova Delays Presidential Vote In Parliament]</ref>

====Aftermath====
A new [[2012 Moldovan constitutional referendum|constitutional referendum]] was announced to be planned for April, with the presidential election to be held afterwards. On 15 January, the leaders of AEI made a joint statement announcing that a constitutional referendum would be held in April. Prime Minister Vlad Filat said that "a referendum on the amendment of the constitution will be initiated in order to give people the opportunity to rectify the constitutional deficiencies that have been triggering endless political crises." Acting President [[Marian Lupu]] added that: "Moldova's president will be elected within a month after the validation of the referendum's outcome." Liberal leader [[Mihai Ghimpu]] then said that the amendment to the constitution is the only solution to the political crisis.<ref>[[Moldpres]], [http://www.moldpres.md/News.aspx?NewsCod=379 Moldovan ruling alliance wants to amend constitution via referendum] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230731183835/https://www.moldpres.md/News.aspx?NewsCod=379 |date=31 July 2023 }}</ref><ref>[[Jurnal de Chişinău]], [http://www.jurnal.md/ro/news/aie-ini-iaza-un-referendum-na-ional-pentru-modificarea-constitu-iei-214748/ AIE inițiază un referendum național pentru modificarea Constituției!] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120120140608/http://www.jurnal.md/ro/news/aie-ini-iaza-un-referendum-na-ional-pentru-modificarea-constitu-iei-214748/ |date=20 January 2012 }}</ref> The leaders of AEI then withdrew their proposal for the referendum during a special address on ''[[Moldova 1]]'' on 10 February. It added that a date for a new presidential vote would be announced on 16 February at the opening of the parliamentary session.<ref>{{cite web |author=Armenia Azerbaijan Belarus Georgia Moldova Ukraine |url=http://www.easternpartnership.org/daily-news/2012-02-13/moldova-scraps-plans-hold-constitutional-referendum |title=Moldova scraps plans to hold a constitutional referendum |publisher=Easternpartnership.org |date=13 February 2012 |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140826115038/http://www.easternpartnership.org/daily-news/2012-02-13/moldova-scraps-plans-hold-constitutional-referendum |archive-date=26 August 2014}}</ref>

===Third attempt===
On 13 March, Nicolae Timofti and MP [[Mihai Godea]], the leader of the [[Democratic Action Party (Moldova)|Democratic Action Party]] talked about the expected election and what would make Godea agree to vote for him.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://portal.moldpres.md/default.asp?Lang=en&ID=168680 |title=Non-affiliated Moldovan MP to decide on support for presidential candidate on 14 March |publisher=Portal.moldpres.md |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120720195305/http://portal.moldpres.md/default.asp?Lang=en&ID=168680 |archive-date=20 July 2012}}</ref> Two days later Godea announced his support for Timofti.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.inprofunzime.md/stiri/politic/godea-s-a-razgandit-va-vota-pentru-nicolae-timofti-maine.html |title=GODEA s-a razgandit! VA VOTA pentru Nicolae Timofti MAINE |publisher=Inprofunzime.md |access-date=18 March 2012}}</ref> At the end of a meeting with Timofti on 14 March, Igor Dodon had not decided whether to back him.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://portal.moldpres.md/default.asp?Lang=en&ID=168749 |title=Moldovan Socialists need more time to decide on support for ruling coalition's presidential candidate |publisher=Portal.moldpres.md |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120712113111/http://portal.moldpres.md/default.asp?Lang=en&ID=168749 |archive-date=12 July 2012}}</ref>

Following meetings between the [[Alliance for European Integration]] and the [[Party of Socialists of the Republic of Moldova]], another election attempt was set for 16 March<ref name=March/> after parliament affirmed the date 9 days earlier, though PCRM boycotted the vote on the premise that the incumbent parliament is illegitimate, amidst their demand it be dissolved.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://english.ruvr.ru/2012_03_07/67803958/ |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120710073949/http://english.ruvr.ru/2012_03_07/67803958/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=10 July 2012 |title=Moldovan Parliament sets presidential election for March 16: Voice of Russia |date=7 March 2012 |access-date=16 March 2012 }}</ref> Three PCRM rebels, who called themselves "socialists," and led by Igor Dodon suggested they would back Timofti. Dodon said: "The election of a president is better than an aggressive campaign for new parliamentary elections which will not solve the political crisis. We must put an end to the crisis and get down to work for the good of the country. That is how an opposition should act and not choose to stage marches and street demonstrations."<ref name="kiev"/> Timofti said: "The European orientation of Moldova must be a priority. This has been the policy of Moldova in recent years and this is the policy that must continue"<ref name="washingtonpost1"/><ref name="afp"/> and that "I am convinced that Moldova has no other future than a European future."<ref name="rferl2">{{cite news|author=RFE/RL's Moldovan Service |url=https://www.rferl.org/a/who_is_moldovas_new_president/24518350.html |title=Profile: Who Is Moldova's New President-Elect? |newspaper=Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty |date=16 March 2012 |publisher=Rferl.org |access-date=18 March 2012}}</ref> The vote was brought forward from the afternoon to the morning. PCRM also threatened to disrupt the vote.<ref name="kiev"/> PCRM supporters protested the election, but former president Vladimir Voronin later said that the party had called for a suspension to the protests.<ref name="afp"/>

====Final result====
Timofti was elected with the support of AEI, three former PCRM deputies who had defected to PSRM and [[Mihai Godea]]. The head of the election commission, [[Tudor Deliu]], announced the result saying that "after 917 days, the epic of electing a president is over."<ref>{{cite news|last=Tanas |first=Alexander |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-moldova-president-idUSBRE82F19M20120316 |title=Moldova breaks political gridlock, elects president |work=Reuters |access-date=21 March 2012}}</ref> Addressing reporters after the [[Constitutional Court of Moldova]] confirmed him as head of state on 19 March, Timofti said that his inauguration would likely take place on 23 March.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.kyivpost.com/news/russia/detail/124552/ |title=Moldova's new president to be sworn in on March 23 |publisher=Kyivpost.com |date=16 March 2012 |access-date=21 March 2012}}</ref> According to article 79 of the constitution, he must be sworn in as president within 45 days.<ref name="rferl2"/>

