==Reactions==

===Domestic===
Upon being elected, Timofti called for the removal of all Russian peacekeeping troops from Transnistria and said that he advocates a peaceful solution to the [[Transnistria conflict]] through negotiations with the [[5+2 format]] (Russia, Ukraine, the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe, the European Union and the U.S.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.georgiatimes.info/en/news/73264.html |title=New Moldovan President demanded Russia to withdraw its troops from Transnistria – News |publisher=GeorgiaTimes.info |date=14 March 2012 |access-date=18 March 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120506171535/http://www.georgiatimes.info/en/news/73264.html |archive-date=6 May 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref> He also called for reforms and upholding the "rule of law"<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.allmoldova.com/en/moldova-news/1249052774.html |title=Moldova's news – Newly elected President pledges to ensure rule of law |publisher=Allmoldova.com |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120722200010/http://www.allmoldova.com/en/moldova-news/1249052774.html |archive-date=22 July 2012}}</ref> adding that in his role as president and thus "guarantor of the constitution" he would "supervise how the principle of separation of powers in the state is observed as this is a fundamental, democratic principle without which we cannot speak about veritable rule of law. They now speak a lot about the imperfection of the constitution and the necessity of amending it...I will provide all the support for improving the constitutional framework so that there are no blockages in the future and the supreme law ensures the efficient and harmonious development of the country."<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.allmoldova.com/en/moldova-news/1249052772.html |title=Moldova's news – Nicolae Timofti in favor of amending Constitution |publisher=Allmoldova.com |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120718181727/http://www.allmoldova.com/en/moldova-news/1249052772.html |archive-date=18 July 2012}}</ref> He was also congratulated by parliament.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.allmoldova.com/en/moldova-news/1249052766.html |title=Moldova's news – Moldovan parliament congratulates new-elected president |publisher=Allmoldova.com |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120718171338/http://www.allmoldova.com/en/moldova-news/1249052766.html |archive-date=18 July 2012}}</ref>

The AEI also expressed regret over the PCRM's boycott with PM Vlad Filat saying the coalition has sought to "establish a civilised dialogue with the opposition Communists, but they refused to contribute to the efforts of the government. I wish I would have had half of the possibilities that the opposition has now, when I was in opposition. Once the president is elected, the political establishment will have to cope with the hardest burden – we should have a united society at the next parliamentary election, without the barricades that the Communists have built now."<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.allmoldova.com/en/moldova-news/1249052763.html |title=Moldova's news – Moldovan ruling alliance regrets Communists' absence from presidential polls |publisher=Allmoldova.com |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120720051747/http://www.allmoldova.com/en/moldova-news/1249052763.html |archive-date=20 July 2012}}</ref> However, PCRM MP [[Sergiu Sîrbu (politician)|Sergiu Sîrbu]] said the boycott will continue and that "something serious should happen for us to return to parliament, like the resignation of the president or of the government." He also added that a new law that would suspend salaries to those MPs who are absent from sittings of parliament or of parliamentary commissions without reason would not deter the boycott as "it will be a serious blow, but democracy and the fight for justice [would continue]."<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.allmoldova.com/en/moldova-news/1249052776.html |title=Moldova's news – Communist MPs do not give up boycott |publisher=Allmoldova.com |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120718061923/http://www.allmoldova.com/en/moldova-news/1249052776.html |archive-date=18 July 2012}}</ref>

===International===
*{{flag|European Union}} – The [[President of the European Commission|President]] of the [[European Commission]] [[José Manuel Barroso]] also congratulated Timofti on behalf of the EC.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://europa.eu/rapid/pressReleasesAction.do?reference=MEMO/12/196&format=HTML&aged=0&language=EN&guiLanguage=en |title=Message of congratulations from President Barroso to Mr Nicolae Timofti, President of the Republic of Moldova |work=Europa (web portal) |access-date=21 March 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.egovmonitor.com/node/48162 |title=Message of Congratulations From President Barroso To Mr Nicolae Timofti, President of the Republic of Moldova |publisher=Egovmonitor.com |access-date=21 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130122055818/http://www.egovmonitor.com/node/48162 |archive-date=22 January 2013}}</ref> [[President of the European Parliament]] [[Martin Schulz]] congratulated Timofti saying he was "happy that the Moldovan people finally have a president after a two-and-a-half-year long institutional stalemate."<ref>{{cite web |url=http://enpi-info.eu/maineast.php?id=28343&id_type=1&lang_id=450 |title=Schulz welcomes election of Moldovan president to end 2.5-year stalemate |publisher=Enpi-info.eu |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140826114048/http://enpi-info.eu/maineast.php?id=28343&id_type=1&lang_id=450 |archive-date=26 August 2014}}</ref> The [[European Parliament]]'s permanent rapporteur on Moldova Sir [[Graham Watson]] commented: "The Moldovan Parliament and the new President-elect Nicolae Timofti both deserve our congratulations. A three-year political deadlock has finally been resolved. I welcome the emphasis that President Timofti has already put on European integration."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.grahamwatsonmep.org.uk/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=1408&catid=7&Itemid=157 |title=MEP Watson congratulates newly elected Moldovan President Nicolae Timofti |publisher=Grahamwatsonmep.org.uk |date=16 March 2012 |access-date=21 March 2012}}</ref>
*{{flag|Council of Europe}} – [[Secretary General of the Council of Europe|Secretary General]] [[Thorbjørn Jagland]] also congratulated Timofti.<ref name="timpul1">{{cite web|url=http://www.timpul.md/articol/felicitarile-curg-garla-nicolae-timofti-felicitat-de-thorbjrn-jagland-victor-ianucovici-si-victor-orban-32322.html |title=Felicitările curg gârlă! Nicolae Timofti, felicitat de Thorbjørn Jagland, Victor Ianucovici şi Victor Orban |publisher=Timpul.md |date=14 March 2012 |access-date=18 March 2012}}</ref> President of the [[Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe|Parliamentary Assembly]] [[Jean-Claude Mignon]] issued a statement that read: "I am convinced... all the political parties will show responsibility by accepting the democratic legitimacy of the country’s institutions and hence the legitimacy of a president elected in accordance with its constitutional requirements."<ref name="washingtonpost1"/><ref name="afp"/>
*{{flag|Georgia}} – [[President of Georgia|President]] [[Mikheil Saakashvili]] said the election was seen "as a victory for democracy and European values in Moldova."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://unimedia.md/?mod=news&id=45629 |title=Saakaşvili: Alegerea președintelui în Republica Moldova este o victorie a democraţiei |publisher=Unimedia.md |access-date=21 March 2012}}</ref>
*{{flag|Hungary}} – Prime Minister [[Viktor Orbán]] congratulated Timofti over a phone call on 16 March.<ref name="timpul1"/>
*{{flag|Lithuania}} – President [[Dalia Grybauskaitė]] send a message to Timofti that read: "I would like to assure you that Lithuania remains fully committed to your country's endeavors towards integration into Europe."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.15min.lt/en/article/world/lithuanian-president-congratulates-german-and-moldovan-president-elects-529-204684#axzz1ph6D2Kd7 |title=Lithuanian president congratulates German and Moldovan president-elects |publisher=15min.lt |access-date=21 March 2012}}</ref>
*{{flag|North Korea}} – [[Rodong Sinmun]] reported on 30 March, "[[Kim Yong Nam]], president of the Presidium of the DPRK Supreme People's Assembly, sent a congratulatory message on 27 March to Nikolai Timofti on his election as president of the Moldovan Republic. Kim in the message wished the president success in his work for the prosperity of his country, expressing belief that the bilateral relations would develop favorably".
*{{flag|Romania}} – President [[Traian Băsescu]] congratulated Timofti over a phone call on 16 March.<ref name="washingtonpost1"/><ref name="afp">{{cite web|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5i0xLVWfPszBGvUtCY3QNMdTvz7XA?docId=55369b0ff9f1460badc4125f0c2f396b |title=The Associated Press: Moldova elects pro-European judge as president |access-date=16 March 2012}}{{dead link|date=June 2024|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> The president then congratulated the [[Chişinău]] political class for reaching an agreement on the matter. Băsescu also assured Timofti of his full support for continuing the country's internal reform processes and closeness to the EU<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.bucharestherald.ro/politics/34-politics/31514-nicolae-timofti-new-president-of-moldova-basescu-congratulates-him-over-the-phone |title=Nicolae Timofti, new president of Moldova. Basescu congratulates him over the phone! |publisher=Bucharestherald.ro |date=16 March 2012 |access-date=21 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120723102318/http://www.bucharestherald.ro/politics/34-politics/31514-nicolae-timofti-new-president-of-moldova-basescu-congratulates-him-over-the-phone |archive-date=23 July 2012}}</ref> and invited Timofti to visit Romania.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.allmoldova.com/en/moldova-news/1249052771.html |title=Moldova's news – {{Sic|hide=y|Newly|-}}elected Moldovan president receives congratulations |publisher=Allmoldova.com |access-date=18 March 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120907190654/http://www.allmoldova.com/en/moldova-news/1249052771.html |archive-date=7 September 2012}}</ref>
*{{flag|Russia}} – Members of the lower house of [[Federal Assembly of Russia|parliament]], the [[State Duma]], welcomed the election of the president on 16 March. The chairman committee on [[Commonwealth of Independent States|CIS]] affairs and relations with compatriots, [[Leonid Slutsky (politician)|Leonid Slutsky]], said that "Moldova today has finally officially ended a long-term government crisis. Moldova is the world’s only republic which has lived so long without a president."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.itar-tass.com/en/c32/367991.html |title=Duma members welcome election of Moldova president | date=16 March 2012 |language=ru |publisher=Itar-tass.com |access-date=18 March 2012}}</ref>
*{{flag|Ukraine}} – [[President of Ukraine|President]] [[Viktor Yanukovych]] congratulated Timofti during a phone conversation on 16 March and also invited Timofti to visit Ukraine.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/124422/ |title=Yanukovych congratulates Timofti on election as Moldovan president |date=16 March 2012 |publisher=Kyivpost.com |access-date=18 March 2012}}</ref>

